NAME
    hwcount - count host CPU instructions, cycles, and IPC for a window

SYNOPSIS
    hwcount --from TEXT [--to TEXT] [--per N] [--units FILE]
            [--functions N] [--period N] -- <program> [args...]

DESCRIPTION
    Linux-only command of the emusen.dev module. Launches <program>,
    monitors its standard error, and records hardware performance
    counters between the first stderr line containing --from and the
    first subsequent line containing --to (or process exit). Program
    stderr passes through to hwcount's stderr; program stdout follows
    the report.

    The report consists of whitespace-delimited records designed for
    grep(1) and awk(1):

        window seconds S per N ms_each M
        thread TID NAME instr I cycles C ipc R branches B misses M
        sampled thread TID samples N lost L
        unit instr I cycles C ipc R share P% NAME
        function instr I cycles C ipc R share P% NAME
        exit STATUS

    Each thread row counts a group of four user-mode perf_event_open(2)
    hardware events (cycles, instructions, branches, branch misses)
    opened on every thread alive when --from matched. Threads spawned
    later are not counted. On CPUs with fewer than six programmable
    counters, the kernel multiplexes and scales group totals by active
    time.

    When --units FILE is provided, the program's primary thread is also
    sampled every --period cycles and every --period instructions.
    Sampled addresses resolve against program symbols
    (nm -n --defined-only -C), /tmp/perf-<pid>.map (for JIT code,
    removed on exit), 'compiled ?' for unmapped anonymous code, or the
    backing library filename. Unit files contain 'pattern  unit' lines
    (two spaces separating pattern and unit name, '#' for comments);
    the first matching pattern wins, with unmatched addresses classified
    as 'compiled' or 'other'. Unit instruction and cycle counts are
    estimated from sample shares times counted thread totals.

    A program launched via 'systemd-run --scope' preserves its PID and
    can be wrapped directly by hwcount. Sapphire's unit table is located
    at Sapphire/examples/scripts/units.txt.

OPTIONS
    --from TEXT
        Required substring on stderr that opens the measurement window.
    --to TEXT
        Substring on stderr that closes the measurement window (default:
        program exit).
    --per N
        Divide all counts by N and compute ms_each (for example,
        --per 200 across 200 fields).
    --units FILE
        Enable instruction/cycle sampling and map addresses to units
        using FILE.
    --functions N
        Include the top N individual symbols/places by sample count.
    --period N
        Sampling period in cycles and instructions (default: 1,000,000).

EXAMPLES
    dianash -c 'hwcount --from "frame 9100 at" --to "frame 9300 at"
        --per 200 --units units.txt --functions 40 -- bench game ...'
    dianash -c 'hwcount --from start --to end -- prog' | grep '^thread'

EXIT STATUS
    0   Measurement window completed (the program's own exit code is
        reported in the 'exit STATUS' row).
    2   Invalid options, program failed to start, window never opened,
        or kernel refused perf_event_open(2).

SEE ALSO
    dupes(1), profile(1), grep(1), awk(1)
